Chia timelord on macOS — Full Disk Access
==========================================

Required only for topology local-external (CHIA_ROOT on {{ chia_darwin_db_volume }}).
local-internal and remote-node keep CHIA_ROOT on the internal disk ({{ chia_root }})
and do not need this grant.

Why
---
LaunchDaemons run outside a user GUI session and have no Transparency, Consent,
and Control (TCC) grants of their own. A job whose program is a script is
attributed to its shebang interpreter. These wrappers use {{ chia_darwin_interpreter }}.
Until that interpreter has Full Disk Access, the job can see that {{ chia_darwin_db_volume }}
is mounted and still fail to read {{ chia_darwin_db_marker }}. The wrapper then
loops on "waiting for {{ chia_darwin_db_volume }}" and the log's ls= line is
"Operation not permitted". An interactive SSH session can read the same path
while launchd cannot. That mismatch is TCC, not a missing mount.

How to grant it
---------------
1. GUI (this Mac):
   System Settings -> Privacy & Security -> Full Disk Access -> add
   {{ chia_darwin_interpreter }} (Cmd-Shift-G if it is hidden) and enable it.
   Then restart the LaunchDaemons (launchctl kickstart -k system/<label>).

2. MDM: install the sibling profile
   {{ chia_darwin_pppc_dest }}
   through user-approved MDM or Automated Device Enrollment.
   Opening the .mobileconfig locally does not grant SystemPolicyAllFiles
   on modern macOS.

Do not edit TCC.db. It is SIP-protected, and writing it does not grant
Full Disk Access to launchd jobs.

Scope
-----
The grant is for {{ chia_darwin_interpreter }} itself (identifier path,
code requirement: {{ chia_darwin_pppc_code_requirement }}). Any other
LaunchDaemon that starts through that interpreter can then read protected
files, including external volumes. That is the interpreter the running
deployment uses. This role will not swap in a different interpreter or a
custom Mach-O stub.

Companion binaries
------------------
For local-external, also add the launchd-run chia-exporter and chia-healthcheck
binaries to Full Disk Access. They live in {{ chia_darwin_companion_bin_dir }}
(default ~/opt/bin under the service user). The interpreter grant alone can
still leave them failing with "open .../config.yaml: operation not permitted".
This role cannot grant TCC Full Disk Access.

This role only writes this note and the profile template. It never runs
`profiles`, `tccutil`, or SQL against TCC.db.
